  "finalReply": "Answer `D` written to `/app/answer.txt`.\n\nAnalysis:\n- Query filters `price > 50` (~90% of 100k rows) and `stock_quantity > 0` (~99% of rows) — both low-selectivity, so indexes on them (A, B) don't reduce I/O; verified planner still chose Seq Scan + Sort (~42ms) with a price index.\n- Composite index (C) leads with `category_id`, which isn't filtered on — unusable.\n- The expensive step in `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` is the `Sort` (top-N heapsort over ~90k matched rows) for `ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 100`.\n- Index on `created_at` (D) lets Postgres scan in order and stop after 100 matching rows via Nested Loop + Memoize, dropping execution from ~39ms to ~0.18ms with no Sort node. Other options (E,F,G,H,I) are semantically wrong, diagnostic-only, negligible, redundant (PK already indexed), or change results.",
